            <section class="hero">
                <p class="hero-label">Aerospace · CFD</p>
                <h1>CFD Analysis of a<br />de Laval Nozzle</h1>
                <p class="hero-desc">
                    Designed and analyzed a converging-diverging (de Laval) nozzle using
                    Siemens NX, SpaceClaim, ANSYS Meshing, and ANSYS Fluent, to
                    characterize the transition from subsonic to supersonic flow
                    through the throat.
                </p>
                <div class="skill-tags">
                    <span class="skill-tag">Siemens NX</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">SpaceClaim</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">ANSYS Meshing</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">ANSYS Fluent</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">CFD</span>
                </div>
            </section>

            <p class="section-label">Problem / Goal</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    A de Laval nozzle accelerates flow from subsonic to
                    supersonic by narrowing to a throat and then diverging —
                    the same geometry behind every rocket engine bell. The
                    goal was to design a nozzle geometry, generate a mesh
                    accurate enough to resolve the transonic region at the
                    throat, and run a compressible-flow simulation to confirm
                    the flow actually behaves the way isentropic nozzle
                    theory predicts, from subsonic inlet through supersonic
                    expansion.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Approach &amp; Key Decisions</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    Geometry was built in Siemens NX and cleaned up in
                    SpaceClaim before meshing. The mesh itself was
                    unstructured tetrahedral, with local refinement
                    concentrated near the throat — that's the region where
                    velocity and pressure gradients are steepest, and a
                    coarse mesh there would smear out the exact behavior
                    (shock structure, expansion fan) the simulation was meant
                    to capture. Keeping the rest of the domain coarser kept
                    cell count, and therefore solve time, manageable.
                </p>
                <div class="image-placeholder">
                    <img
                        src="/images/de-laval-nozzle/de-laval-nozzle-mesh.png"
                        alt="Nozzle mesh, refined near the throat"
                    />
                </div>
                <p>
                    In ANSYS Fluent, the solver was configured as
                    density-based rather than pressure-based, since
                    density-based solvers handle the strong compressibility
                    and shock-capturing this flow regime requires more
                    robustly. Boundary conditions were set for a subsonic
                    inlet and supersonic outlet to match the physical
                    problem — flow enters slow and pressurized, and should
                    leave the diverging section already supersonic.
                </p>
                <div class="image-row">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/de-laval-nozzle/inlet-velocity-magnitude.png"
                            alt="Inlet velocity contour"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/de-laval-nozzle/outlet-velocity-magnitude.png"
                            alt="Outlet velocity contour"
                        />
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Results / Data</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    Flow accelerated through the throat and expanded
                    supersonically in the diverging section, consistent with
                    isentropic nozzle theory. Mach contours confirmed the
                    expected shock structure, and the velocity contours below
                    show the expansion carrying through cleanly from inlet to
                    outlet with no unexpected separation or recirculation.
                </p>
                <div class="image-row">
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/de-laval-nozzle/mach-contour.png"
                            alt="Mach contour showing supersonic expansion"
                        />
                    </div>
                    <div class="image-placeholder">
                        <img
                            src="/images/de-laval-nozzle/velocity-contour.png"
                            alt="Velocity contour through the nozzle"
                        />
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Lessons Learned / What's Next</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    Mesh quality near the throat mattered far more to result
                    fidelity than overall cell count — refining the right
                    region was more effective than refining everything. This
                    workflow (NX/SpaceClaim geometry into ANSYS meshing and
                    Fluent) is the same compressible-flow pipeline that
                    carries over to modeling actual rocket motor nozzles, and
                    is a natural next step once there's real motor geometry
                    and chamber conditions to simulate against.
                </p>
            </div>

            <footer>
                <p>Marcus David Yang</p>
                <a href="https://github.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github ↗</a>
            </footer>
        </div>

